The structural problem

Three fields account for the vast majority of clones:

- qualified_name: String — 23 sites. Cloned in every tool that builds output structs (calls, refs, similar, change_signals, trace, conventions, components).
- .id.clone() on Convention/Constraint/Component — 30 sites. Every finding/violation construction clones it.
- antecedent: Vec<String> / consequent: Vec<String> on Convention — 21 sites. Entire vectors cloned into every violation struct.

These are all identity data — set once from the DB, never mutated, read and cloned everywhere. The pattern is "agent fights the type system by cloning repeatedly" rather than designing the right ownership model.

Recommended fix, ranked by impact

1. Arc<str> for identity strings (~53 clones → free ref-count bumps)
SymbolRow.qualified_name, FileRow.path, Convention.id, Constraint.id, and their downstream output struct fields. The DB layer constructs Arc<str> once from the sqlite row; every consumer pays zero. Single biggest win.

2. Arc-wrap Convention/Constraint structs (~21 clones eliminated)
Violation structs hold Arc<Convention> instead of cloning id + antecedent + consequent individually. These structs are immutable after construction — perfect Arc candidates.

3. HashMap<i64, &str> for lookup maps (~15 clones eliminated)
The path_map, comp_name_to_id, file_to_component patterns all build owned HashMaps where the source data outlives the map. Borrow instead of clone.

4. trace.rs: track Vec<i64>, resolve names at output (~7 clones eliminated)
The DFS only uses the i64 id; the String is carried purely for formatting. Also stack.last().unwrap().clone() where only the i64 (which is Copy) is used — trivial fix.

5. Borrow-checker fights (~8 sites)
- self.rebuild(&self.symbol_attrs.clone()) — partial-borrow workaround, fix with mem::take or restructure
- self.conventions.clone() returned from &mut self — return &[Convention] instead
- self.constraint.clone().into_iter() — just .iter()
- stack.last().unwrap().clone() for destructuring — just .0

Honest assessment

The bulk of the problem isn't "bad code" — it's missing a design decision. When the codebase was written, everything was String because it's the path of least resistance, and then every consumer pays the clone tax. The fix is one architectural choice (Arc<str> for identity fields) that propagates through the DB layer and eliminates ~2/3 of all clones mechanically. The borrow-checker fights are noise by comparison.

